course: medicine
lesson: 7
title: Colds and flu
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A cold is an infection of the nose and throat by one of more than 200 viruses; the runny nose is your body washing the viruses out.
explain: You catch a cold from other people's viruses, not from wet hair or cold weather; colds are commoner in winter mostly because people crowd together indoors.
explain: Antibiotics kill bacteria, not viruses, so they do nothing for a cold, even when the snot turns green.
explain: Flu is caused by influenza viruses and is much worse than a cold: it comes on suddenly, with high fever and aching all over, as if you'd been hit by a bus.
explain: There is no cure for a cold; rest, drinks and time, about a week to ten days, are what get you better.
fact: A cold is an infection of the nose and throat by one of more than 200 different viruses, and it gets better on its own in about a week to ten days.
fact: Antibiotics do not work on colds, because colds are caused by viruses, not bacteria.
fact: Wet hair and cold weather do not create cold viruses; colds are commoner in winter mostly because people are indoors close together.
fact: Green or yellow snot is coloured by white blood cells fighting the infection and happens in ordinary viral colds too.
fact: Flu comes on suddenly with high fever, aching muscles, headache, shivers and great tiredness.
fact: The UK children's flu vaccine is a nasal spray with weakened viruses that cannot cause flu; it changes every year because flu viruses change.
fact: Flu spreads by droplets from coughs and sneezes and on hands and surfaces, and older people and babies can get very ill from it.
fact: Whether you have a cold or a fever, the body needs fluids and energy; starving a fever is not a good idea.
fact: Honey can soothe a sore throat and calm a cough a little, but it is never given to babies under 12 months.
fact: A cough can last weeks after a cold, but a cough lasting more than three weeks should be checked by a GP, a family doctor.
fact: A child with a mild cold and no fever can usually go to school.
fact: Taking vitamin C every day does not stop most people catching colds; it might shorten a cold slightly in some studies.
exercise: What causes a cold? | One of more than 200 viruses | Going out with wet hair in winter | Eating too much ice cream | The cold weather itself, nothing else
exercise: Why are colds commoner in winter? | People are indoors close together | Cold air turns into viruses | Wet hair freezes the inside of the nose | Days are shorter and darker
exercise: Your snot went green. Do you need antibiotics? | Usually not; green snot happens in viral colds too | Yes, green always means you need antibiotics now | Yes, and double the usual dose | No, green snot means you are already cured
exercise: Why don't antibiotics help a cold? | Colds are caused by viruses, not bacteria | Colds are too small to be treated by medicine | Antibiotics only work in the summer months | Colds are caused by bacteria that hide
exercise: How is flu different from a cold? | It comes on suddenly with high fever and aches | It is just a very bad runny nose | It is caused by bacteria, not by a virus | It lasts only an hour, then stops all at once for good
exercise: What is in the children's flu spray? | Weakened flu viruses that can't cause flu | Real flu that gives you a short mild flu | A strong dose of vitamin C | Salt water only, to clean the nose
exercise: Why does the flu vaccine change every year? | Flu viruses change | The old one goes off in the fridge | Children get a bit bigger each year | Nobody knows, it is just tradition
exercise: Dad says "feed a cold, starve a fever". What's the truth? | With either, the body needs fluids and energy | Never eat or drink anything with a fever | Eat as much as you can with a cold | Drink nothing at all while you have a high fever
exercise: Who must never be given honey? | Babies under 12 months | Children over five years old | Grandparents with a cough | People who have a cold
exercise: When should a cough after a cold be checked by the GP? | When it lasts more than three weeks | After one day, whatever it's like | Never, coughs always go by themselves | Only if it is a dry tickly cough
exercise: Why did grandma get flu after your friend had it? | Flu spreads by droplets and hands to others | Grandmas get flu from a cold wind | Flu jumps between houses by itself at night | She ate the same lunch as him
exercise: You have a sniffle and no fever. Can you go to school? | Usually yes | No, stay home for a whole week | No, all colds mean home | Only if you wear a scarf
exercise: Do vitamin C tablets stop you catching colds? | Not for most people | Yes, every single time | Yes, if you take ten a day | No, they give you colds
exercise: How long does a cold usually last? | About a week to ten days | About an hour or two | About six months, sometimes longer | For ever, it never really goes
exercise: Who can get very ill from flu? | Older people and babies | Nobody, flu is always mild | Only children at school | Only athletes who train hard
exercise: Why does your nose run with a cold? | The body is washing the viruses out | Your brain is slowly melting | You drank too much water that day | The nose is crying from the pain
test: nan reckons I'll get a cold going out with wet hair. true or nah? | Nah, colds come from viruses spread by people | Yeah, wet hair makes the viruses grow | Yeah, but only in winter | Nah, colds come from cats and dogs mostly
test: Sam's snot's green so his mum wants antibiotics. What would a GP most likely say? | Wait it out; most green snot needs no antibiotics | Take antibiotics straight away, before it spreads | Green snot needs a small operation | Take your sister's leftover pills
test: "I've got flu" says Olly, but he's playing football with a runny nose. Is it likely flu? | No, real flu usually puts you in bed | Yes, flu is just a bit of a sniffle | Yes, playing football in the cold causes flu | No, it is hay fever for sure
test: What would happen if healthy kids never had the flu spray? | Flu could spread more easily to babies and grandparents | Children would become immune to all illness | Nobody would ever get flu again | Colds would disappear from schools
test: Aunt says she never catches colds because of her vitamin C pills. What's wrong with her thinking? | Research shows vitamin C doesn't prevent most colds | Nothing, she is right, it has been proven | Vitamin C actually causes colds | She must have the flu instead and just doesn't know it
test: The flu spray at school, will it give me flu? | No, the weakened viruses cannot cause flu | Yes, a small flu for about a week | Yes, but only in the nose | No, because it is just water and nothing else
test: In a cartoon, a boy is given an antibiotic for his cold and is better in one second. What's wrong? | Antibiotics don't work on viruses, and colds take days | Antibiotics actually work even faster than that | Colds can't be cured by anything but magic | Nothing, that is exactly right
test: Mum gives honey and lemon for my cough. Can she give it to my 6-month-old brother too? | No, honey is never given to babies under one | Yes, honey is safe for all ages | Yes, babies need even more honey than you | No, it's the lemon that is the danger for babies
test: Why do people catch more colds after the summer holidays end? | Children mix closely indoors again | The colder weather makes new viruses | School food gives colds | Shorter days shrink the immune system
test: Two kids have the same cold. One goes to school with no fever, one stays home. Who followed the advice? | Both can be fine; a mild cold with no fever is usually OK at school | Only the one at home, every cold means a day off | Only the one at school, no one may stay home | Neither, both must see a doctor
test: My cough's lasted a month and I'm wheezy. Just a long cough? | Get it checked by a GP | Yes, ignore it and it goes | Yes, coughs last a year | No, it means a broken rib
test: If all colds come from viruses, why can't doctors just make one cold vaccine? | There are more than 200 different cold viruses | Colds are not real illnesses | Vaccines don't work inside noses | They already did and it is kept secret
exam: Identify the reason for which antibiotics are ineffective in the treatment of the common cold. | The common cold is caused by viruses rather than bacteria | The common cold is too mild to warrant any medicine | Antibiotics are ineffective during the winter months | The common cold is caused by exposure to cold air rather than organisms
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises influenza as compared with the common cold? | Influenza begins abruptly with high fever, aching and exhaustion | Influenza is a milder variant of the common cold | Influenza is caused by bacteria rather than by any kind of virus | Influenza is confined to the nose
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the nasal influenza vaccine administered to children. | It contains weakened viruses incapable of causing influenza | It induces a mild course of influenza lasting one week | It consists solely of vitamin C | It requires no revision from year to year
exam: It is asserted that daily ingestion of vitamin C prevents colds. State the conclusion supported by research. | Daily vitamin C does not prevent colds in most people | Daily vitamin C prevents all colds | Daily vitamin C causes colds in children | Daily vitamin C removes any need for rest during illness
